<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicola Pisano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1220–c. 1284</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicola Pisano was an Italian sculptor who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and is often seen as a founder of modern sculpture. His pulpits for the Pisa Baptistery, finished in 1260, and Siena Cathedral drew on Roman sarcophagi to give figures new weight and realism. With his son Giovanni he carved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontana-maggiore">Fontana Maggiore</a> in Perugia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicola-pisano.jpg" alt="Nicola Pisano" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pio Fedi (1816-1892), Italian sculptor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicola_Pisano,_by_Pio_Fedi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His birth date and origins are uncertain. The archives of the Cathedral of Siena record him as the son of Petrus de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, which points to Apulia, and he was probably trained in the workshops of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, whose coronation he attended. His only surviving works from this period are two griffon heads. Around 1245 he moved to Tuscany to work at the castle of Prato, whose portal lions are probably by him, and he then worked on the façade of the Cathedral of Saint Martin in Lucca, where the Deposition from the Cross, the Nativity and the Adoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a> are attributed to him. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> between 1245 and 1250, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pisano">Giovanni Pisano</a> was born there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 1255 he received the commission for the pulpit of the Pisa Baptistery, which he finished and signed Nicola Pisanus in 1260, helped by assistants including Arnolfo di Cambio and Lapo di Ricevuto. Its hexagonal parapet holds five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> marble reliefs from the life of Christ, and the figures wear Roman dress. He probably knew Roman sarcophagi in the Camposanto of Pisa, among them the Phaedra sarcophagus, and the nude Fortitude derives from a Roman Hercules. Between 1260 and 1264 he completed the work of the architect Diotisalvi on the dome of the baptistery. In 1264 he was asked to design the Shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-dominic">Saint Dominic</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, though his own part was probably small.</p><p>In September 1265 he was commissioned to make a marble pulpit for Siena Cathedral, carved between the end of 1265 and November 1268 with his son Giovanni and assistants including Arnolfo di Cambio. It resembles the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> pulpit but is larger and is considered his masterpiece; its subject is salvation and the Last Judgement. In July 1273 he was commissioned to make the altar of San Jacopo in Pistoia Cathedral, demolished in 1786. His last major commission was the reliefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontana-maggiore">Fontana Maggiore</a> at Perugia in 1277–1278, designed by Fra Bevignate and Boninsegna, where most of the carving was done by Giovanni and assistants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pisano was no mere imitator of antiquity: his figures are original creations, formed by close study of ancient models. Surveys of Italian Renaissance art often begin in 1260, the year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> pulpit, and his works are counted among the most important precursors of Renaissance sculpture. He also remained attached to Gothic art, and the Siena pulpit shows this. Arnolfo di Cambio is seen as the true inheritor of his classical style, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pisano">Giovanni Pisano</a> mixed French Gothic with classical elements. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> included a life of Nicola Pisano in his Lives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicola Pisano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Elected priors ruled from the Palazzo dei Priori, and the Fontana Maggiore by <b>Nicola</b> and Giovanni Pisano was completed in 1278.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-perugia">Commune of Perugia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Foggia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Tuscany, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.46,15.55&amp;year=1261" aria-label="Foggia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4929 686 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicola Pisano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5379 956h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5379 956h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.46,15.55&amp;year=1261">What was here in 1261? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
